  "rm": false,
  "notes": "First Q-simple, geometrically split example of [16] for the census (the gsplit cell was empty). The curve is y^2 = 36106861993*q2(x)*q4(x) with q2 = 1329879x^2 + 2641584x - 4867136 and q4 = 5196415497521787x^4 - 16762391873288622x^3 + 36294227199512151x^2 - 10927535587547712x + 28509243963738332. Construction: quadratic-conjugate (2,2)-gluing over K = Q(sqrt(-87)) of E = E_{2,8}(t) (HLP Table 3) at t = (z - 1)/4, z = (-1 + 3*sqrt(-87))/22, where E(K) has a point P of order 8 and full 2-torsion; E is glued with its conjugate along the moving transposition of the 2-torsion labels, and the exact square condition a(z)b_0(z^sigma) = ((20958 + 4354*sqrt(-87))/14641)^2 (a(z) = -z(z^2 - 1), b_0(z) = -z(z^2 + 1)(z^2 - 2z - 1)) lifts (P, sigma P) to a rational point of order 16 (the order-8 point of the elliptic parent gains a factor 2). Exact checks: the divisor D above has 16D = 0 and 8D = [q2/1329879, 0] != 0; good reduction with #J(F_5) = 32 and #J(F_23) = 528 bounds the torsion by 16; chi_23 = T^4 - 2T^2 + 529 is irreducible, so J is simple over Q; the involution (x, y) -> ((x - 16/3)/(2x - 1), (29*sqrt(-87)/9) y/(2x - 1)^3) of C over K has a genus-1 quotient, so J is geometrically split (the geometric automorphism group has order 4).",
